# C# Style

- Project-local patterns and formatter settings take priority.
- Use explicit access modifiers. Use `PascalCase` for types, methods,
  properties, events, and public members; `camelCase` for locals/parameters;
  `_camelCase` for private instance fields.
- Name booleans with `Is`, `Can`, or `Has`; use `TryX` for fallible operations.
- Use `[SerializeField] private` for Inspector references/tunables and preserve
  serialized names or provide an explicit migration.
- Keep per-frame callbacks small. Put deterministic gameplay rules in plain C#
  classes; use MonoBehaviours for lifecycle, presentation, and scene binding.
- Use ScriptableObjects for shared authored definitions/catalogs, not as a
  wrapper for every value.
- Prefer precise responsibility names over `Manager`, `Helper`, or `Utility`.
- Use `var` when the type is obvious; use braces for all control flow.
- Comments explain constraints and why, not what readable code already says.
- Never log every frame. Log actionable transitions and failures.
- Follow the project's established async stack. Avoid `async void` except
  framework event handlers, and handle exceptions there.
- Do not edit generated, package-cache, vendored, or third-party code.

